format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radiation-and-the-body

objective u4-three-ways

statement:
    Know the three ways to lower a dose: spend less time near the source, stay farther from it, or put a shield between you and it.

misconception: Nothing can be done to lower a dose.

item u4-l4-choice-distance
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-protecting-yourself

prompt:
    You move farther away from a source. What happens to the dose?

options:
    {"id":"down","label":"It decreases, and not just a little"}
    {"id":"same","label":"No change, because radiation is everywhere","note":"Radiation from a source gets weaker with distance. The dose decreases dramatically as you move away."}
    {"id":"up","label":"It goes up","note":"Moving away lowers the dose."}

answer: down

explanation:
    The dose of radiation decreases dramatically as you increase your distance from the source.

item u4-l4-choice-time
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-protecting-yourself

prompt:
    A worker spends less time near a source. What happens to the dose from that source?

options:
    {"id":"less","label":"It goes down: less time means less dose"}
    {"id":"same","label":"It stays the same","note":"Limiting the time you are exposed reduces the dose from the source."}
    {"id":"more","label":"It goes up","note":"It is the other way round. Spending less time near a source lowers the dose."}

answer: less

explanation:
    Limiting the time you are exposed reduces the dose from the radiation source.

item u4-l4-fill-three
type: fill
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-protecting-yourself

prompt:
    The three ways to lower a dose are time, distance and ___.

mode: bank

bank:
    {"id":"s","label":"shielding"}
    {"id":"c","label":"cooling"}
    {"id":"w","label":"weighing"}

answer: s

explanation:
    Shielding means putting something, such as lead, concrete or water, between you and the source.

item u4-l4-tom-nothing
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-protecting-yourself

statement:
    There is nothing a person can do to lower a dose from a source.

answer: myth

explanation:
    Myth. People lower a dose with less time near the source, more distance from it, and shielding between themselves and it.

item u4-x-choice-material
type: choice
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-protecting-yourself

prompt:
    Which of these can shield against penetrating gamma rays?

options:
    {"id":"good","label":"Lead, concrete or water"}
    {"id":"paper","label":"A sheet of paper","note":"Paper stops alpha particles, but gamma rays pass through it. They need thick, dense material."}
    {"id":"skin","label":"The top layer of skin","note":"Skin stops alpha particles. Gamma rays are very penetrating."}

answer: good

explanation:
    Barriers of lead, concrete or water protect against penetrating gamma rays.
